# @VERSION@ - install guide

This is a build of the accessibility layer you can run and listen to.

<!-- windows -->
This guide is for **Windows**. The Mac has its own download, with its
own guide.

You install **two files**, and they go in **two different folders**:

- `zzz_accessibility.pk3` - the mod itself, into the game's folder.
- `autoexec.cfg` - binds the three keys, into your Documents folder.

Section 2 does both. Neither replaces or modifies anything you already
have, and uninstalling is deleting the two of them.
<!-- /windows -->
<!-- mac -->
This guide is for the **Mac**. Windows has its own download, with its
own guide.

You install one file, `zzz_accessibility.pk3`, into ET: Legacy's folder
in your Library, and `autoexec.cfg` beside it only if you want to change
the keys. Section 2 does both. Neither replaces or modifies anything you
already have, and uninstalling is deleting them.
<!-- /mac -->


## 1. What you need first

1. **ET: Legacy @ENGINE_TAG@**, from https://www.etlegacy.com/download.
   The version matters: these libraries were built against @ENGINE_TAG@
   and will not match a different release. Section 2 shows how to check
   which one you have before you install anything.
2. **The original Wolfenstein: Enemy Territory assets** - `pak0.pk3`,
   `pak1.pk3`, `pak2.pk3` in `etmain`. ET: Legacy's installer can fetch
   these, or you can copy them from an existing Wolfenstein: Enemy
   Territory install. They are
   not mine to distribute.
<!-- windows -->
3. **Optional: NVDA.** Without it, speech falls back to SAPI 5, which is
   built into Windows and needs nothing installed. See step 3.
<!-- /windows -->
<!-- mac -->
3. **Nothing else.** The game speaks through VoiceOver when it is on,
   and through the Mac's own voice when it is off. ET: Legacy runs on
   Apple Silicon and Intel Macs alike.
<!-- /mac -->


## 2. Install the accessibility build

<!-- windows -->
You downloaded one file, `@ARCHIVE@`. Unzip it anywhere - it extracts
into a single folder of the same name, containing this guide, the mod,
the config, and a checksum list.

**First, check this build matches your game.** Look in the `legacy`
folder of your ET: Legacy installation for a file named
`legacy_v<something>.pk3`. This build is for **@ENGINE_TAG@**, so that
file should read `legacy_@ENGINE_TAG@.pk3`. If it reads a different
version, stop - these libraries were compiled against @ENGINE_TAG@ and
are not the right build for your engine.

Two things then get copied out of that folder, to two different places.

### Step one: the mod

Copy `@PACKAGE@` into the `legacy` folder of your ET: Legacy
installation, next to the file named `legacy_@ENGINE_TAG@.pk3`. For a
default install that is:

    C:\Program Files\ETLegacy\legacy\@PACKAGE@

`legacy` is the mod ET: Legacy runs by default, so there is no command
line flag to set.

### Step two: the config (optional now)

**You can skip this step.** From this release on, the mod binds F5, F6
and F8 for you the first time it runs, so the keys work with step one
alone. In the previous release they did not, and a missing config file
was indistinguishable from a broken mod.

Copy the file anyway if you want to change which keys are used or retune
the cue sounds - it is the easiest place to do both, and section 5
explains it. Copy `autoexec.cfg` into:

    Documents\ETLegacy\legacy\autoexec.cfg

**This is not the same folder as step one.** The mod goes where the game
is installed; the config goes in your Documents folder, which is where
ET: Legacy keeps your settings. Both folders are called `legacy`, which
is confusing, and they are not the same place.

**If an `autoexec.cfg` is already there, do not overwrite it.** Open
both in Notepad and paste the new lines onto the end of the one you
have.

If you already had F5, F6 or F8 bound to something of your own, the mod
leaves yours alone and does not claim that key. In that case copy the
file and change the binds in it to keys you do not use - section 5 shows
how.

### That is the installation

Start the game. Menus should narrate straight away; in a match, F5
reports your status.

Section 5 covers changing the keys and retuning the cue sounds, which is
optional. Everything required is above.

<!-- /windows -->
<!-- mac -->
You downloaded one file, `@MAC_ARCHIVE@`. Open it: the Mac unzips it
into a single folder of the same name, containing this guide, the mod,
the config, and a checksum list.

**First, check this build matches your game.** This build is for ET:
Legacy **@ENGINE_TAG@**: the version is in the name of the file you
downloaded from etlegacy.com. If yours is a different version, stop -
these libraries were compiled against @ENGINE_TAG@ and are not the right
build for your engine.

### Step one: the mod

1. Start ET: Legacy once and quit it, if you never have: that is when it
   makes its folder for your settings and mods.
2. In Finder, choose **Go**, then **Go to Folder** (Command Shift G),
   type `~/Library/Application Support/etlegacy/legacy` and press
   Return.
3. Copy `@PACKAGE@` from the unzipped folder into that folder.

`legacy` is the mod ET: Legacy runs by default, so there is nothing to
set.

### Step two: the config (optional)

**You can skip this step.** The mod binds F5, F6 and F8 for you the
first time it runs.

Copy `autoexec.cfg` into the same folder as `@PACKAGE@` only if you want
to change which keys are used or retune the cue sounds; section 5
explains it. **If an `autoexec.cfg` is already there, do not overwrite
it.** Open both in TextEdit and paste the new lines onto the end of the
one you have.

If you already had F5, F6 or F8 bound to something of your own, the mod
leaves yours alone and does not claim that key.

### Step three: bots (optional)

To play against bots in a game you host, two things are needed.
ET: Legacy's own Mac version comes without bot support, so the mod
brings a game module built with it, `qagame_mac`, and the bots
themselves are Omni-bot, which comes from its own page.

1. In a web browser, open https://github.com/jswigart/omni-bot/releases/latest
   and download the file whose name ends in `_ET.zip`, for example
   `omni-bot_0_93_ET.zip`. Open it: it unzips into a folder holding one
   folder called `omni-bot`.
2. Move that `omni-bot` folder into the same `legacy` folder, beside
   `@PACKAGE@`, as it is on Windows. The game finds it there by itself,
   with nothing to set; your home folder works too.
3. Start the game. Once the update check has answered, the game sees
   Omni-bot, fetches the `qagame_mac` for this version, checks it, puts
   it in the legacy folder, and says *"Bots file updated for this
   version."* This needs the internet once; without it, copy
   `qagame_mac` from this download into the legacy folder yourself.

Then set how many bots play on the Hosting page (Options, Accessibility,
Hosting), and start a server. If something stops the bots, the game says
so a few seconds into the match: no bots file, a bots file from another
version, or no Omni-bot in the legacy folder.

When the game updates the mod, it updates `qagame_mac` too, the next
time it starts, and says so.

Omni-bot's own guide talks about a config file and `omnibot_path`. You
need neither: the mod turns Omni-bot on when you host with bots, and
tells it where its folder is.

If you host with bots, the game says nothing is missing, and still no
bots come, the Mac may be refusing a file downloaded from the web that
no developer signed. In Terminal, run this once, as one line:

    xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine "$HOME/Library/Application Support/etlegacy/legacy"

### That is the installation

Start ET: Legacy. With VoiceOver on, the game speaks through VoiceOver;
with it off, through the Mac's own voice. In a match, F5 reports your
status.

Most Mac keyboards have no number pad, and the marker keys are on it.
Options, Accessibility, Key Binds moves any of them to a key you have:
arrow to a row, press Enter, then press the key.

<!-- /mac -->
### Two notes on the pk3

The file name begins with `zzz` on purpose. ET: Legacy sorts the pk3
files in a mod folder and searches the last one first, so a name that
sorts after `legacy_@ENGINE_TAG@.pk3` takes priority. Renaming it to
something earlier in the alphabet will silently stop it working.

The pk3 contains the libraries for 32-bit and 64-bit Windows and for
the Mac, Apple Silicon and Intel alike. The game loads the ones that
match your computer, so the same file is correct everywhere - which is
also why the in-game updater has one file to offer everybody.


<!-- windows -->
## 3. NVDA, or the SAPI fallback

Speech prefers NVDA and falls back to SAPI 5 when NVDA is not there.

**The file NVDA needs is in this download now.** It used to be a
separate errand - find NV Access's controller client, work out which
architecture you have, put the DLL in the right place - and searching
the web for a DLL by name leads to sites that serve tampered copies,
which for somebody who cannot see the screen is about the worst thing
that can land on their machine. So the real file is in the `nvda`
folder beside this guide, taken from NV Access, checked against a
checksum recorded in the repository, with their own licence text.

Copy **one** of these next to `ETLegacy.exe`, where the game is
installed:

    nvda\x64\nvdaControllerClient.dll   for 64-bit ET: Legacy
    nvda\x86\nvdaControllerClient.dll   for 32-bit ET: Legacy

If you are not sure which you have, take the x64 one; it is what a
normal install of ET: Legacy is. The wrong one simply does not load,
and you get SAPI instead - it cannot break anything.

You do not need the file at all if SAPI suits you. Nothing else stops
working without it.

With NVDA running, narration goes through your own voice, rate and
punctuation settings, and queues with the rest of your screen reader
output instead of talking over it. Without the DLL, or with NVDA closed,
you get SAPI instead, which speaks independently of your screen reader.
SAPI lets each line finish too, and the next waits its turn. If the game
says more than SAPI can keep up with, it drops the oldest lines waiting
rather than falling behind. A key you press for an answer cuts in at
once, as it does with NVDA.

The choice is made per message, so starting or quitting NVDA mid-game is
picked up on the next line spoken.


<!-- /windows -->
<!-- mac -->
## 3. VoiceOver, or the Mac's own voice

Speech goes to VoiceOver when VoiceOver is on, as announcements that
queue with the rest of what VoiceOver says, in your own voice, rate and
verbosity. When VoiceOver is off, the game speaks through the Mac's own
voice instead, and lets each line finish before the next. A key you
press for an answer cuts in at once either way.

The choice is made per message, so turning VoiceOver on or off mid-game
(Command F5) is picked up on the next line spoken.

Nothing needs installing for either.


<!-- /mac -->
## 4. What should happen

**In the menus**, moving between items speaks the item under focus
*along with what it is currently set to* - "Game Type:, Campaign",
"Friendly Fire, On" - and changing it speaks the new value. Confirmation
dialogs are read out when they appear. Text boxes read back what they
hold and echo what you type; section 6 covers that in full.

**Enter changes the setting you are on.** Arrow to it and press Enter:
a game type steps to the next one, a yes/no or a checkbox toggles, and
the new value is spoken. This needed fixing. Every settings control in
the game asked two questions before it would do anything - do I have
focus, and is the mouse pointer inside me - and from the keyboard the
second can never be true, because arrowing onto a control does not move
the pointer. So Enter did nothing, on every checkbox, every yes/no and
every game type in the game. A tester found it trying to host a server
and being unable to choose the game type.

Clicking is unchanged: a mouse button still only acts on the control
the pointer is actually over.

**Sliders step with the left and right arrow keys.** Arrow to one -
*"Effects Volume"*, *"Mouse Sensitivity"* - and left and right move it,
speaking the new value each time. Twenty or so presses take it from one
end to the other, and the numbers on the way are round ones: the volume
goes in steps of 0.05, the sensitivity in whole numbers, because a flat
twentieth of every range would give you ladders like 1, 2.45, 3.9 to
listen to. Pressing past either end says the value again rather than
going silent, so you can hear that you are already at the top.

A slider left at some odd figure by a mouse snaps onto that ladder on
the first press, and it can never be pushed outside its own range.
Dragging with a mouse still works exactly as before.

**The controls list says which key each control is on, and lets you
change it.** Arrow down the list and each row reads out its name and
its key - *"Crouch, C"*, *"Forward, UPARROW"*, *"Lean Left, Q or
MOUSE4"*. A control with nothing on it says *"not bound"* rather than
the punctuation the screen draws there.

To change one:

1. Arrow to the control and press **Enter**. You will hear *"Crouch,
   press the key to use. Escape to cancel, backspace to clear."*
2. Press the key you want. It is taken immediately and read back -
   *"Crouch, bound to V"*.

There is no third step to confirm, and deliberately so: while the game
is listening, **the next key you press is the binding** - so a
confirming Enter would simply bind Enter. Escape cancels and tells you
what the control is still on; backspace clears it.

If the key you chose was already doing something else, the game takes
it off that control - the same as it has always done for a mouse. The
read-back is the row itself rather than the key you pressed, so it
tells you where things actually ended up.

**Joining a match** goes through the limbo panel, which opens with
**L** once you are on a server. That panel is normally mouse-only, so
this build gives it keyboard controls of its own - you do not bind
anything for this, and there is no console typing:

- **Down arrow** or **Tab** moves to the next choice, **Up arrow** back
  to the previous one. The order is the three teams, then the five
  classes, then "Confirm and spawn".
- Each choice is spoken as you land on it, with its state: *"Axis"*,
  *"Allies, selected"*, *"Covert Ops, unavailable"*, *"Confirm and
  spawn"*. "Full" means the team has no room, "unavailable" means the
  server has limited that class, and "selected" is what you are on now.
- **Enter** picks the choice you are on, then says it again so you hear
  it become "selected".
- After the classes come your two weapon slots - *"Primary weapon,
  Thompson, 1 of 3"*. **Enter** moves to the next weapon in that slot
  and says it. The count is the game's own, so it already accounts for
  your class and your skill level.
- A slot with only one weapon is skipped, because there is nothing to
  choose there. Spectators get no weapon stops at all.
- **Tab** still does what it does in the stock game - swaps which
  weapon slot the panel is showing - and now says which one it landed
  on.

So the whole sequence is: press **L**, arrow down to a team, Enter,
arrow on to a class, Enter, arrow through the weapons pressing Enter to
change one, then arrow to "Confirm and spawn" and Enter.

**In game**, five keys. The mod binds these itself the first time you
run it, so they work whether or not you copied `autoexec.cfg`. Section 5
covers picking different ones. No console typing is involved.

It does not take a key you already use - with one exception. **9** and
**0** are bound in the stock game to weapon banks 9 and 10, and neither
can do anything: the engine refuses any bank above 9, and bank 9 is
empty. They are the only keys near the movement hand that cost nothing
to take, so the mod claims those two and nothing else that is in use.

**9** says your health. **0** says the rounds loaded in the weapon you
are holding and how many are left in reserve. Those two sit under the hand already on the movement keys,
because a firefight does not wait for you to reach the F row.

**F5** reports everything at once - health, weapon, ammo, objective,
distance and direction. **F6** says the last thing again, which in a menu
means the item you are on. **F8** cuts speech off.

**O**, the objectives key, reads the map's objective description for
your team - the same text the panel shows - and then where the current
objective is. Nothing was rebound for that; the key you already press
just says more.

If a map has no description, or no current objective, it says so rather
than going quiet. Silence and breakage should never sound alike.

F5 speaks, in order: your health, your current weapon, your ammo, the
name of your team's current objective, how far away it is in metres, how
far above or below you it is, and whether to turn left, turn right, or
continue straight ahead toward it.

**Up or down is said.** An objective on a roof or an upper floor - the
Oasis command post, for one - is not reached by walking at it: "ahead"
alone takes you to the wall underneath. So when it is two metres or
more above or below you, the report adds *"Objective is 4 meters above
you"*. On the way, the marker's distances say it the same way: *"25
meters to marker. Marker is 4 meters above you"*.
Whatever F turns you to is said the same way - *"Command post, ahead
left, 12 meters, 4 meters above you"*. Within two metres of your own
level - the same floor, a step or two off it - nothing is added.

F6 matters more than it sounds. A menu item is spoken once, when focus
lands on it, so a name lost under another sound is otherwise only
recoverable by moving focus away and back - and on a menu with a single
item, not at all. F8 matters for the opposite reason: an objective
report runs for several seconds and nothing else interrupts it.

**Changing weapon** says the weapon, how many rounds are loaded, and how
many are left in reserve - *"Colt, 8 loaded, 24 in reserve"*. A knife or
a grenade reports only what it has. This fires however the weapon
changed: a number key, the mouse wheel, running dry, or spawning.

**If you get wedged** on scenery while trying to move, the mod notices
and reads out the objective, how far it is and which way to turn - the
same thing the **O** key gives you. It only speaks when you are actually
pressing a movement key and getting nowhere, so standing still to listen
or to aim will not set it off, and it says it once rather than
repeating.

**Audio cues** play on their own: a low health cue below 25 health, a
cue when you can interact with something, a directional cue when you
take damage telling you where it came from, and an objective cue with a
direction when you respawn.

The cues are the game's own sounds, played through the engine, so they
follow your volume settings and mix with the game instead of talking
over it. The two directional cues are placed in the world, so the engine
positions them the way it positions any other sound.

Each cue is a setting you can change, and section 5 shows where. These
are the defaults:

    eta_cueLowHealth            sound/player/gasp.wav
    eta_cueObjective            sound/misc/referee.wav
    eta_cueInteraction          sound/menu/select.wav
    eta_cueDamageDirection      sound/weapons/impact/flesh1.wav
    eta_cueObjectiveDirection   sound/misc/vote.wav

Those defaults are a guess. Nobody has heard whether a gasp reads as
"low health" or whether the interaction blip is long enough to notice
mid-firefight, so they are the first thing worth changing by ear. Any
path from the game's own sound folders works.


## 5. Changing the keys, and the cue sounds

Nothing here is required. Section 2 already put `autoexec.cfg` where it
needs to be, and the defaults work. This section is for changing them.

The game has a built-in console, and most instructions for games like
this one tell you to type into it. Don't bother: it is a text field the
game draws itself, with no screen reader support at all. Edit the file
instead, in any editor you get on with:

<!-- windows -->
    Documents\ETLegacy\legacy\autoexec.cfg
<!-- /windows -->
<!-- mac -->
    ~/Library/Application Support/etlegacy/legacy/autoexec.cfg
<!-- /mac -->

The engine runs that file every time the game starts, after it loads
your saved settings, so what is in it wins. Save, restart the game, and
the change is live.

### What the file does

Two things, and it is commented throughout so you can read it.

First, it binds three keys:

    bind F5 accessibilityStatus
    bind F6 accessibilityRepeat
    bind F8 accessibilityStopSpeech

Those are the only things the mod cannot do for itself. Any keys work -
open the file, change them, save, restart.

Function keys rather than letters, on purpose: the game's own default
config binds every letter from A to Z, so a letter taken here silently
steals something. An earlier version of this guide used **H**, which is
`dropobj` in the stock config - dropping the objective you are carrying.
F5, F6 and F8 are unbound by default. F7 is not.

If your keyboard has a keypad, most of it is spoken for now:
`KP_SLASH` is the scope key, the arrow cluster and Keypad 5 walk the
map, and `KP_INS`, `KP_DEL`, `KP_STAR` and `KP_PLUS` are the marker's
keys. **Period**, which turns you toward the way to the marker, was the
game's `mapzoomin`.
`KP_INS` only looked free in any case - the game's left-handed layout
uses it.

Second, it names the five cue sounds:

    seta eta_cueLowHealth          "sound/player/gasp.wav"
    seta eta_cueObjective          "sound/misc/referee.wav"
    seta eta_cueInteraction        "sound/menu/select.wav"
    seta eta_cueDamageDirection    "sound/weapons/impact/flesh1.wav"
    seta eta_cueObjectiveDirection "sound/misc/vote.wav"

Those are already the built-in defaults, so the cues work whether or not
you copy the file. They are written out so you can change one and hear
the difference: edit a path, save, restart the game, listen. That loop
is how the cues get tuned by someone who can actually hear them.

Each of those five sounds is one the game already ships and already uses
elsewhere, so nothing extra is downloaded. If you point a cue at a file
that isn't there, it plays silence and says nothing about it - so change
one at a time.

### If you would rather use the console

It is still there. It opens with **Shift and Escape** together, or with
the tilde key below Escape. Shift+Escape is the more reliable of the
two, because the tilde key sits in different places on different
keyboard layouts.

Typing the same lines there works and takes effect immediately, which is
useful for trying a cue sound without restarting. It just won't read
back to you.

## 6. What to know before you play

**Joining a server used to be a trap, and is not any more.** On the
join-server screen the game rebuilt menu focus from the mouse cursor
every time a key was pressed. With a hand on the mouse that is what you
want. From the keyboard alone the cursor never moves, so it won every
time: you could arrow to "REFRESH LIST", hear it announced, press Enter,
and connect to whichever server happened to be highlighted in the list.
A tester lost a session to exactly that. This build leaves keyboard
focus alone until the mouse actually moves, so Enter now does what the
narrator just told you it would.

**Most public servers switch the mod off.** Many online servers run
`sv_pure`, which checks that your game files match the server's. On one
of those the game quietly loads its own files instead of the mod's, so
you join, and the speech stops. Nothing here gets round that, on
purpose: `sv_pure` is how servers keep cheats out. A game you host
yourself, and a friend's server with `sv_pure 0`, keep the mod on - the
next part is how.

**Hosting a game, with bots or with friends.** Open **Options**, then
**Accessibility**, then **Hosting**. Four rows:

- *Friends join with the plain game* - Yes by default. Your server
  checks nobody's game files and sends out no downloads, so a friend
  joins with the ET: Legacy they already have, and does not go home with
  a game that talks. You keep the mod: it is your own server. Set it to
  No and the Host Game screen's own *Pure Server* and *Client Downloads*
  are used as they are.
- *Bots* - how many bots play: None by default, or 1 to 19. Exactly
  that many, shared between the teams, however many friends join.
  Changed during a match you host, from the menu Escape opens, they join
  or leave at once - *"6 bots now"*. In a match you started with none,
  they come with the next map.
- *Bot skill* - Easiest to Hardest. Easy by default.
- *Lock on to enemies by itself* - Yes by default. In a game you host,
  an enemy coming into sight is locked on to without a key; see Aiming,
  by itself and with a key, below.

Then **Host Game** from the main menu: choose the map, and press
**Start Server**. The settings are applied as the server starts. Leave
*Auto-Configure Server* at No - the competition settings it offers turn
the pure check back on.

The bots are Omni-bot, which the ET: Legacy installer puts in the
`legacy\omni-bot` folder. If no bots have come half a minute after the
map loads, Omni-bot is missing from your installation: install ET:
Legacy again with its own installer.

**What a friend needs.** Just ET: Legacy, the same version as yours.
Before joining they set **Options**, **System**, *Get Missing Files* to
*Disabled* - otherwise their game asks your server for the mod, your
server says no, and the game gives up on joining. Or in their console:
`cl_allowDownload 0`. On the same network your game shows up in their
server list under LAN. Over the internet, they connect to your address
in the console - `connect` and your IP - and your router has to pass
UDP port 27960 through to your computer.

**Joining a friend's server.** It keeps the mod on if they host with
*Pure Server* set to No on the Host Game screen's Advanced page, or
`sv_pure 0` in their server's config.

**The scope announces itself however you reach it.** Going to a rifle's
scope is a weapon change in this game - the scoped FG 42 is a different
weapon from the plain one - and for a while that broke the announcement.
The scope line went out the moment you scoped, then the weapon change
finished a fraction of a second later, the mod announced *that* too, and
speech cut the first line off to deliver the second. You heard the
weapon name you already had, and nothing about the scope or the view.
The binoculars never had the problem because raising them changes no
weapon.

Scoping is now treated as the scope going up, not as picking up a
different weapon, so the report finishes: *"Scope"*, then what your view
is pointed at. Coming out says *"Zoom off"*. Hearing neither means the
scope did not engage - the game refuses it while prone and moving, for
instance.

Which key you use makes no difference. The mod reads the game's own zoom
state, not the key, so the weapon-bank number keys, `weapalt`, and the
mouse all announce identically.

**A keypad key for `weapalt`.** Separately from the above: `weapalt` -
which sets a mortar or a machine gun down and detonates a satchel, as
well as scoping a rifle - is bound in the stock game to the **right
mouse button and nothing else**. It is the only action in the default
config with no keyboard key at all. This build also binds it to **keypad
slash**, under the same rule as the mod's own keys: only if that key is
free. Rifles can be scoped from the number keys without it; a mortar
cannot reliably be set without it.

**Obstacles are only announced when they actually stop you.** This one
was reported and was wrong. The mod used to trace a line from your eyes
and name whatever it found within a couple of metres — which sounds
reasonable and answers the wrong question. A line from your eyes tells
you what you are *looking at*; what matters is what is *stopping you*.

Those are different constantly. Every stair has an upright face, so a
line finds a "wall" on every staircase in the game while you climb it
without trouble. Every corridor has walls beside it. And the distance
was a whole number, so anything closer than a metre came out as *"0
metres"*.

Now the mod asks the engine the question you care about — can I move
forward — by sweeping your own body's shape ahead of you, lifted at the
bottom by exactly the height the game lets you step up. A stair passes
underneath it, the way your feet do. If your body can move, nothing is
said. If it cannot, you hear what is in the way: *"Stone wall"*,
*"Door"*. No distance, because at that point it is always nothing.

That was still only half a fix, and the same tester found the other
half. The probe had the right *shape* and the wrong *aim* — it still
pointed where you were looking, so running down a corridor while
glancing sideways announced the wall for as long as you ran. It is
aimed by your movement keys now, turned by your view: the direction you
are asking to go. Nothing can block you in a direction you are not
trying to move in. And it says nothing at all unless you are asking to
move and not getting anywhere, which is what being blocked means.

**The objectives are called in the map's own order.** This matters most
when you are playing alone, and it is what a tester found by spending a
match walking between things that could not be started yet.

ET's maps run their objectives in sequence — you cannot blow the second
wall before the first one is down. The mod used to point you at
whichever objective was *nearest*, which on most maps is the wrong one.
It now follows the map's own numbered list, so the status key and the
objectives key both open with where you are in it:

> *"Objective 2 of 4: destroy the side wall."*

And when one is finished or lost, you hear it as it happens — *"objective
2 of 4, complete"* — which is the stamp a sighted player watches land on
the objective panel. When every one is done you hear that too, because
silence at that moment reads as the narration having given up.

The numbering, the order and the three states are the game's own: a
map's script sets them and your objective panel shows the same thing.
The mod is reading them, not deciding them.

**There is an Accessibility menu in the Options menu.** Everything the
mod adds is behind it, and until now the only way to change any of it
was to edit `autoexec.cfg` in a text editor.

**Options → Accessibility** is the last button before BACK, so from the
Options menu it is one press of Up and then Enter — or Tab down to it
like anything else. It opens a short menu of its own, laid out like the
Options menu: four buttons and BACK.

- **Environment** — what the mod tells you about the world around you.
  Sixteen yes/no rows: objectives, surroundings, openings as you walk,
  players near you, things on the ground, direction, location, the kind
  of place, what you can use, the command map survey,
  whether the map key opens and closes the map, door sounds,
  construction, demolition and flag sounds, crosshair sounds and the marker
  sound. Enter toggles each one and the new value
  is read back.
- **Speech** — how the voice sounds. Four sliders, stepped with the left
  and right arrow keys: how far the music steps back while the narrator
  is talking, and the speech rate, pitch and volume for the system's own
  voice.
- **Key Binds** — every key the mod answers, twenty-five bind rows, listed
  below.
- **Hosting** — what happens when you host a game yourself: friends
  joining with the plain game, bots, how well the bots play, and the
  enemy lock working by itself. Section
  6 has the whole of hosting.

Escape or BACK on any of the four pages returns you to that short menu,
not all the way out. The game's own CONTROLS page is exactly the one the
game ships.

The Key Binds page:

- **Look and turn** — turn left and turn right (the mod's: a tap steps
  to the next compass point, a hold turns smoothly), look up, look down,
  and smooth turn left and right, plus the "Tap turns to next point"
  setting. The look and smooth-turn rows are the *game's* own commands,
  not the mod's, and the game's own controls list has no row for any of
  them anywhere — stock ET aims with the mouse and assumes everyone can.
- **Spoken reports** — status, health, ammo, say-that-again, stop
  speaking.
- **Command map survey** — all eight.
- **Your marker** — place it, ask where it is, clear it.

Each row says the key it is on, and Enter starts the capture — *"Status
report, press the key to use. Escape to cancel, backspace to clear."*
As on the game's own controls page, a row holds two keys: a new key is
added beside the one already there, and once there are two, the next
one replaces both. To move a control to one new key, clear the row with
Backspace first, then press Enter and the key.

Until this build none of these rows could actually be changed. They
read out the right key, and pressing a new one did nothing, because the
game's menus only let you change a key for a command on the game's own
built-in list, and none of the mod's were on it. A tester found it
trying to move Look Down.

That is the answer to "which keys are free". Every letter from A to Z is
bound in the stock game — H is *drop objective*, Z is *fireteam message*
— which is why the mod's keys ended up on function keys and the keypad.
You do not have to take anybody's word for it: pick a key, and if it was
doing something else you will hear that control say *"not bound"* the
next time you pass it.

**Resetting the controls no longer takes these keys away.** The DEFAULT
button on the CONTROLS page execs the stock config, which binds what the
game ships with and drops everything else — including every key this mod
added. Until now that was permanent: the mod remembers which keys it has
offered you and does not offer them twice, so one press of DEFAULT took
the status key, the look keys and the whole map survey away for good,
with nothing to say why. It now puts them back as part of the same
reset. If you hit that on an earlier build, the console command
`accessibilityInstallBinds` is the way back.

One of the game's own menus is shadowed by this: the Options page, for
its ACCESSIBILITY button, because there is no other way to put a button
on it. A test fails the build if ET: Legacy ever changes that menu, so
the copy cannot quietly go stale.

**The music gets out of the narrator's way.** A tester turned the music
up and found the narrator underneath it. That is not a badly chosen
volume on either side — it cannot be fixed by any slider, because the two
are mixed by different things that cannot see each other. The music is
the game's. The speech goes out through the system, by way of your screen
reader or the system's own voice, and never passes through the game's
mixer at all. So turning the music up
really does bury the narration and nothing stops it.

So the mod does what broadcast has always done: it dips the music while
the voice is on it, and brings it straight back. It is the game's own
music fade — the same one map scripts use — so it costs nothing and
sounds like the game doing it.

    seta eta_musicDuck "0.3"

1 is the default and means off, because this moves audio you set up
yourself. 0.3 leaves the music clearly there underneath; 0 silences it
while words are being spoken. If a map fades its own music down, that is
respected: the dip is a fraction of whatever the map asked for, and the
music returns to the map's level rather than to full.

How long it stays out of the way is an estimate from the length of the
line, and it has to be: no screen reader tells a program when the words
have stopped. It is biased slow on purpose, and the music rises
gently rather than snapping back, so an estimate that runs out a little
early is heard as the music returning and not as a click.

<!-- windows -->
**The narrator's voice — SAPI 5 only.**

    seta eta_speechRate   "0"
    seta eta_speechPitch  "0"
    seta eta_speechVolume "100"

Rate and pitch run from -10 to 10, with 0 the voice's own default; volume
is a percentage.

**These do nothing if you use NVDA**, and that is deliberate rather than
a gap. NVDA's controller library has three entry points — is it running,
say this, stop — and none of them carries a rate, a pitch or a volume,
because NVDA speaks at the settings *you* chose in NVDA. A game quietly
overriding your own screen reader would be a bug, not a feature. Change
them in NVDA and the mod follows. They are here for SAPI 5, the fallback
when NVDA is not running, where there is no such preference to respect.

If you are on NVDA and the narrator is hard to hear, `eta_musicDuck`
above is the setting that will help.

<!-- /windows -->
<!-- mac -->
**The narrator's voice — the Mac's own voice only.**

    seta eta_speechRate   "0"
    seta eta_speechPitch  "0"
    seta eta_speechVolume "100"

Rate and pitch run from -10 to 10, with 0 the voice's own default; volume
is a percentage.

**These do nothing while VoiceOver is on**, and that is deliberate:
VoiceOver speaks at the settings *you* chose in VoiceOver Utility, and a
game quietly overriding your own screen reader would be a bug. They are
for the Mac's own voice, used when VoiceOver is off.

If you are on VoiceOver and the narrator is hard to hear, `eta_musicDuck`
above is the setting that will help.

<!-- /mac -->
**How loud things are: Accessibility → Sound.** A page of its own, apart
from Speech, which is the screen reader's. Each runs from 0, silent, to
100, the level it always had:

    seta eta_markerVolume    "100"   // the marker's ping
    seta eta_siteVolume      "100"   // construction, demolition, flag and tank sounds
    seta eta_doorVolume      "100"   // door creaks
    seta eta_ladderVolume    "100"   // ladder knocks
    seta eta_crosshairVolume "100"   // the crosshair tick
    seta eta_cueVolume       "100"   // low health, something to use, damage, objective direction
    seta eta_pickupVolume    "100"   // health packs, ammo packs and weapons on the ground
    seta eta_announcerVolume "100"   // the game's own announcer
    seta eta_voiceVolume     "100"   // players' and bots' voice chats

On the page they are *"Marker volume"*, *"Objective sounds volume"*,
*"Door sounds volume"*, *"Ladder sounds volume"*, *"Crosshair sounds
volume"*, *"Alert sounds volume"*, *"Pickup sounds volume"*,
*"Announcer volume"* and *"Voice chat volume"*. The
announcer is the map's recorded voice - *"The bridge has been
destroyed"*, *"Dynamite planted"*. The voice chats are players and bots
calling out - *"Medic!"*, *"Need ammo"* - and their words are still read
in the chat. Turn either down if it drowns out the marker. Switching a sound off altogether is still on Environment.

**Settings take effect as you change them.** They used not to. Every one
of the mod's settings was read once, when the map loaded, and never
again — so turning one off from the console stored the new value, showed
it back to you, and changed nothing at all until the next map. Fixed
here, and pinned by a test, because there was no way to tell from inside
the game that it was happening.

**The arrow keys turn and look, and say what you end up pointing at.**
Stock ET has no key for either. Aiming is the mouse and nothing but the
mouse — there is no `+left`, no `+right`, no `+lookup` and no `+lookdown`
anywhere in the default config. So the compass could tell you that you
were facing south and leave you with no way to act on it.

- **Left** and **Right** turn on the spot. A **tap** turns to the next
  of the eight compass points, fast, and says it the moment it lands —
  *"north-east"* — the way a flick of the mouse does; two taps turn a
  quarter. **Holding** the key turns smoothly and stops where you let
  go, for aiming.
- **Up** and **Down** tip the view.

The tap exists because the keys turn at the game's fixed speed, 140
degrees a second: a quick tap moves the view about fifteen degrees, a
third of a compass point, so it used to take three or four taps before
there was a new point to name. The mod finishes the turn for you by
pressing the game's own turn key and the key that makes turning faster,
under key numbers no keyboard has, and letting go on the point. The one
setting it touches, `cl_anglespeedkey`, is put back as soon as the step
ends and is never saved to your config. "Tap turns to next point" on
Accessibility → Key Binds, or `eta_turnSteps 0`, makes the keys turn
smoothly however they are pressed; Smooth Turn Left and Right are on
the same page for a separate pair of keys.

Up and Down only started working in a match in beta-0.00.7. ET:
Legacy's game code deletes the look-up and look-down commands every time
a match loads - the engine even has an exception written in so that it
may - and nothing brings them back until the game restarts. So the keys
worked in the menus and did nothing in play. The mod now leaves them
alone.

The compass names each point **as you cross it** while the key is held —
*"north-east"*, *"east"*, *"south-east"* — so you can hold the key and
let go on the heading you want. While a turn key is held it says each
point as soon as the view is properly inside it, rather than waiting for
the view to rest there as it does for the mouse. That took a fix: the callout used to
wait for a new point to be held for 350 ms, and a held key moves the
view through a point in 321 ms, so the wait never completed and the
compass said nothing at all until you released. A 29 ms miss that made
the feature silent for the entire time it was needed.

**The kind of place is said as you walk.** The tester asked to follow
the streets and alleys and tell the parts of a building apart. As you
come into a new kind of place, you hear it:

- *"Street, north to south, 8 meters wide"* - a way between buildings,
  and which way it runs.
- *"Alley, east to west"* - a narrow one.
- *"Square"* - an open space with walls round most of it, and *"Open
  ground"* when there are none.
- *"Indoors"*, and *"Indoors, upper floor"* when there are rooms under
  you.
- *"Balcony"* - a narrow place up on a building, open to the sky.
- *"Rooftop"* - a broad one.
- *"Ledge, 4 meter drop beside"* - the edge of a drop with solid ground
  under you: a terrace, a wall top, a cliff. Mind the fall.

It is worked out from the map's own walls and floors as you walk. A
street that bends a little, or narrows to an alley, is the same street,
and a doorway or a cart does not make a new one - what is said is what
the last few seconds agree on. Turning into a street that crosses it is
news. Accessibility → Environment → *Kind of place*, or
`eta_placeCallouts 0`, turns it off; F9 still says it.

**F9 tells you what is around you.** A sighted player walking into a
room takes it in at a glance - the ways out, the window, the truck
parked across the street. Press F9 for the same glance, in this order:

- **What kind of place, and where** - indoors or outdoors, what kind of
  place it is, and the map's own name for it when it has one: *"Outdoors,
  alley, east to west, Water Pump"*, *"Indoors, upper floor, Allied
  Command Post"*, or the command map square when the map has no name
  there, *"Outdoors, street, north to south, 8 meters wide, grid C3"*.
- **The enemies in sight**, nearest first, ahead, left, right or behind
  and how far - *"Enemies in sight, ahead left 18 meters"* - or *"No
  enemies in sight"*. Then any **wounded** teammate.
- **The open ways**, by compass point, and how far each runs - *"Open to
  the north 40 meters, east 25 meters, south, far"*.
- **The walls close by** - *"Walls close to the west, north-west"*.
- **What blocks a way**, when it is a thing rather than the map - *"Close
  by, truck to the east"*.
- **Any window**, and how far.
- **The named things near you**, nearest first, each with its distance
  and direction and whether you have a clear line to it - *"truck, 12
  meters north-east"*, *"door, 4 meters west"*, *"crate, 6 meters south,
  out of sight"*.
- **The teammates near you**, by name - *"Teammates near, Bob right 4
  meters"*.

A direction counts as open when it runs more than eight metres, which is
further than across a room or a corridor. The names come from the game:
pickups, chairs and doors by the game's own classification, and
everything else by the name its map maker gave the model file, so a
truck is "truck". Things built into the map's walls and floors have no
name anywhere in the game, so a table that is part of the building is
not in the list; a table the map placed as a separate object is.

The binoculars, the scope, the look keys and the callout when you walk
into something use the same names: the view ending on the truck says
*"Truck"*. The narrator never says *"Object"*. What the game knows by
kind is said as that - *"Mounted machine gun"*, *"Health cabinet"*,
*"Landmine"*, *"Body"*, and a construction or a wall to blow up with the
objective it belongs to: *"Water Pump, construction"*, *"Old City Wall,
dynamite target"*. A part of the map with no name at all - a barrier, an
invisible fence round a construction site - is said as the wall or
ground it is, with the objective close by when there is one: *"Old City
Wall, wall"*.

The left-handed layout opens the stats on F9, so the mod leaves F9 alone
there: give "What's Around Me" a key you can spare on Accessibility →
Key Binds.

**Players near you are said as they come and go.** Without pressing
anything:

- **An enemy** - *"Enemy, ahead left, 18 meters"* - when one comes into
  sight, again as they come closer (*"Enemy closer, left, 7 meters"*)
  or move away, and *"Enemy out of sight, last seen left"* when they
  are gone. Several at once are one line: *"3 enemies, nearest ahead,
  20 meters"*. An enemy shot down is *"Enemy down"*.
- **A wounded teammate**, waiting for a medic - *"Wounded teammate Bob,
  behind, 12 meters"* - and again as you get closer.
- **Teammates** when they come within fifteen metres, and again when one
  is right beside you. A spawn full of them is one line: *"6 teammates,
  nearest Bob, right, 4 meters"*.

Directions are from the way you are facing: ahead, ahead left, left,
behind left, behind, and the same on the right.

An enemy is said only in clear sight - nothing solid between you and
their body or head - and only in front of you, inside the view, unless
they are within ten metres, where you would hear them. Never through a
wall. The game sends your computer the positions of enemies you cannot
see, and the mod does not use them: that would be a cheat. A disguised
covert op is a teammate to you, as they are to a sighted player, unless
you are a Field Ops with the skill that sees through disguises.

**Things on the ground are said as you reach them.** A weapon, ammo or a
health pack within five metres and in sight - *"MP40 on the ground,
ahead right, 3 meters"*, *"Ammo Pack on the ground, ahead, 2 meters"*.
Each is said once, and again only after you have walked away from it
and come back. A covert op also hears an enemy body with its uniform
still on: *"Enemy body with a uniform, left, 4 meters"* - walk to it
and press F: F faces the body and holds the use key for you until the
uniform is on - *"Taking the uniform"*, then *"Uniform taken, you are in
disguise"*. An enemy you have just shot is not a body yet: they lie
wounded, waiting for a medic, and the game leaves the body only once
they give up. Shoot them again and they are gone, uniform and all. To a
covert op, F says them as *"Enemy down, uniform once they give up,
don't shoot"*.

**Furniture and the things in a room are said as you come up to
them.** A chair, an armchair, a bed, a bookcase, a chest of drawers, a
crate, a barrel, a vase, a basket - and a window, when you are inside -
within about a metre and a half and in sight: *"Armchair, ahead
left"*, *"Window, right"*. Each is said once, and again after you have
walked a few metres off and come back; two of the same side by side
are said once. The scope, the binoculars and walking into something
name it the same way: *"Crate"*, not *"Wooden wall"*. They are read
from the map itself, from the names of what each surface is painted
with, so only what the map names is found: a plain table or chair a
map builds from the same wood as its walls is not. On Accessibility →
Environment as *"Furniture and things in a room"*, or
`eta_furnitureCallouts 0` to turn it off.

Enemies come first, then the wounded, then everything else, and nothing
but an enemy talks over another line: a weapon on the floor waits for
the narrator to finish. Both are on Accessibility → Environment:
*Players near you* and *Things on the ground*.

**Aiming, with a key and by itself.** The view turns for you the way a
tap on Left or Right turns to a compass point - onto the thing you can
use when you press F, onto an enemy when one comes into sight - and two
keys do it when you ask: F for everything to use, INS for an enemy.

The view never turns to a thing to use by itself. An earlier version
turned it when you stopped beside one; in a match that pulled the view
away from where you were looking, so it is gone. F does it when you
want it.
- **F, the one key for everything to use.** With something under the
  crosshair the game offers to use, F uses it, as it always has. With
  nothing there, F turns you to the nearest thing you can use within
  fifteen metres. Within reach, it uses it as you arrive - quicker than
  standing still for the turn above. Further off, it says what it is and
  where - *"Water Pump, to build, ahead left, 6 meters"* - and keeps you
  facing it while you walk there. Once you are close enough you hear
  *"Water Pump, to build, in reach, F to use"*; press F and it is used.
  A turn key lets go of it, as below. Press F again within
  four seconds, with still nothing under the crosshair, for the next
  one: *"Door, 2 of 3, right, 3 meters"*. It knows the objectives your
  command map shows you - a construction to build, an enemy one to blow
  up - doors, health and ammo cabinets, mounted guns, things on the
  ground, a wounded teammate, and for a covert op an enemy body with its
  uniform - F takes the uniform, holding use for you - or an enemy down
  who will leave one. With nothing within fifteen metres, F says
  *"Nothing to use within 15 meters"* and is the plain use key. The
  game's icon for something use does nothing with - a crate to shoot, a
  thing to knife, water, a locked door - does not stop F turning you to
  the nearest thing. The mod takes F
  only while it is still on the game's own use command; the left-handed
  layout uses ENTER, which the mod leaves alone - bind it to
  `+accessibilityActivate` for the same.

- **] and [ choose what F turns to.** With several things to use close
  together - a dynamite spot beside a door beside a cabinet - F takes the
  nearest, or whatever the crosshair is already on, which may not be the
  one you want. So:

  1. Press **]**. It says the nearest thing to use, without turning you
     - *"Dynamite target, ahead left, 3 meters, 1 of 4"*.
  2. Press **]** again for the next one, further off, or **[** to go
     back. It goes round the list.
  3. Press **F**. You turn to the one you chose, and if it is within
     reach it is used - whatever the crosshair was on.
  4. Further off, F turns you to it and keeps you facing it while you
     walk: the view follows it as you go. Once you are close enough
     you hear *"MP40, in reach, F to use"*; press **F** and it is
     picked up or used.

  The view lets go when you use it, press a turn key, choose something
  else, or lose sight of it for a moment - *"MP40 is out of sight. F
  turns to it again"*. The choice itself stays, so F turns you back to
  it.

  Something on the ground is in reach when it is in the game's own reach
  from your eyes, a little under two and a half metres, which along the
  floor is about two metres.

  A weapon or a pack lying on the ground between you and what you chose
  would be picked up instead: the game's use takes the first thing in
  front of you. So F aims at a part of what you chose that nothing else
  covers - or, for anything not on the ground, a little higher, over
  it. If that cannot clear it, nothing is pressed, and F says so -
  *"Not used: MP40 on the ground is in the way, and would be picked up.
  Step to one side, then F"*.

  The choice is let go once it is used, once it is gone, or half a
  minute after you last pressed F or a bracket key. To pick something
  else, press ] or [ again. A ladder or stairs is let go once F has
  turned you to it, since F climbs a ladder you are at. The left-handed layout zooms the
  map on [ and ], so the mod leaves them alone there: bind
  `accessibilityUseNext` and `accessibilityUsePrev` to other keys on the
  Key Binds page.

  This used to be two keys - DEL turned to things, F used them. A tester
  asked for one, and F is it. DEL is the mod's no longer: if an earlier
  build put the turn on it, it now does nothing, and you can bind it to
  whatever you like.
- **INS, held - lock on to an enemy.** The nearest enemy in sight is
  said - *"Locked, enemy, ahead left, 14 meters"* - and your sights stay
  on them for as long as you hold the key. Fire as usual. When they go
  down or out of sight the lock takes the next enemy in sight, or says
  *"Lock lost"*. Letting go, or pressing a turn key, stops it.
- **The lock by itself.** Holding a key proved too slow - the enemy had
  the first shot before it was down. So where the lock is allowed, an
  enemy coming into sight within forty metres is locked on to without a
  key, and the next one when they go down. It says nothing of its own:
  the callout has already named the enemy and where they are, and the
  crosshair tick tells you the sights are on them. Press a turn key and
  it leaves you to look away for two seconds; open the map or count out
  a build and it stands down. Accessibility → Hosting → *Lock on to
  enemies by itself*, or `eta_autoLock 0`, turns it off and leaves only
  the held key.

The lock, held or by itself, follows the same rule as the callouts: only
an enemy in clear sight, never through a wall. And it works only in a
game you host -
with bots, or with friends - or on a server whose host has allowed it
by typing `sets eta_aimAssist 1` in the server console. Anywhere else
it says so and does nothing. Keeping your sights on an enemy for you is
aim assistance, and on somebody else's server that would be a cheat
against every player there.

INS is free in the stock layout. The left-handed layout looks down on
INS, so the mod leaves it alone there: bind `+accessibilityAimLock` to a
key you can spare, on Accessibility → Key Binds or in the console.

**The build count is no longer talked over.** While the pliers are
counting a build out, the players-near-you callouts wait for the count
to finish - even an enemy, which otherwise is said at once. A tester
heard no build progress at all once enemies started being called over
it.

**Openings are said as you walk.** While you move, the mod keeps track
of how far the way runs to the north, east, south and west, and says
when a side opens - *"Opening to the east"* at a side street or the
corner of a building - and when the wall comes back - *"Wall to the
east"* as you pass it. Walking out of a street into a square says both
sides at once: *"Opening to the east and west"*. So you hear where a
street begins and where it ends, by compass point.

A side has to stay open for a moment before it counts, so a doorway
walked past says nothing, and nothing is said while you stand still.
The way you are walking and the way behind you are left out: the wall
in front of you is the wall-ahead report's, and the way behind is the
way you came. Accessibility → Environment → *Openings as you walk*, or
`eta_openingCallouts 0`, turns it off.

**The scoreboard, your stats and the top shots speak.** Each is a
window you hold a key to see, and each now says what it shows when you
press its key:

- **Scoreboard** (Tab) - the time left, each team's size and total XP,
  where you stand on your team (*"you are 2 of 6, 45 XP"*), then every
  player's name and XP, your team first.
- **Player stats** - kills and deaths, accuracy, headshots, damage given
  and received, rank and XP, then each weapon you have used with its
  accuracy and kills.
- **Top shots** - the best player with each weapon, with their accuracy
  and kills.

The numbers come from the server a moment after the key is pressed, so
the answer follows a short pause, as the window does. F8 cuts a long
scoreboard off. The keys are the game's own, on its Controls page.

**Every door creaks**, including doors that only one team may use. The
server marks only doors that every team may open, so those used to be
silent; the mod now reads the map's own list of doors instead. A door
your team cannot open creaks the duller, slower way.

**Compass zoom speaks.** Compass Map Zoom In and Zoom Out, on the game's
own Controls page, say where the zoom stands and how much of the map the
compass shows — *"Compass zoom 9 of 14, about 40 meters across"*, or
*"the whole map"* at the widest. Pressing past either end says *"Compass
fully zoomed in"* or *"out"*.

There is no key for putting the view back level, and that is not an
omission — the game has no command for it. ET's own left-handed layout
binds HOME to `centerview`, and `centerview` is registered by nothing
anywhere in ET: Legacy. It has been a dead bind for years, and only the
game's own executable could add a live one; this mod is the cgame and ui
modules. So the report below is how you find level again: hold **Down**
until it tells you the ground is a few metres off.

The compass already names the direction you settle on — *"facing
north-east"*. Up and down get the other half, and it is not the angle.
*"Forty degrees up"* is honest and useless: forty degrees up is the sky
in a field and a ceiling in a cellar, and what you want to know is
which. So the mod traces the line you are actually looking down and says
what is on the end of it:

> *"Open sky."*

> *"Concrete ground, 3 meters."*

> *"Wooden ceiling."*

> *"Nothing in range."*

It is the same trace the binoculars use, along the same collision the
game itself runs, so it never reports through a wall. It waits for the
view to settle before speaking, for the reason the compass does — a
sweep from the floor to the sky passes through everything in between,
and narrating the journey buries the destination. And it stands down
entirely while the binoculars or a scope are up, because that narration
is already answering the same question at the range you chose.

`eta_facingCallouts` covers both halves; set it to 0 for silence about
where you are pointing.

**The command map, square by square.** Press the map key — **G** in the
stock controls, the same key a sighted player uses — and the map opens
and stays open: *"Map open"*. The arrow keys - or the keypad's arrows -
then walk a cursor across the map's own lettered grid, and a second
press of the map key, or Escape, closes it: *"Map closed"*.

While the map is open the keys are the map's. You do not move, turn,
look or fire; the arrows walk the map and nothing else. The mod's own
keys still work - F5 and the other reports, the marker keys, the
survey's Home, End, Page Up and Page Down. Close the map and every key
is back to its usual job. A key you were already holding when the map
opened - running forward, say - still lets go when you let go. With
*Map key opens and closes* set to No, the map shows only while the key
is held and the keys are left alone. Every step says where you are looking, what
is there, and how to get to it:

> *"C,3. Axis command post, two teammates, a street running north to
> south, a building to the east with a balcony and a rooftop. 40
> metres, north-east, 52 degrees."*

> *"C,4. Empty."*

Two things are on the survey that the game's own map leaves out, both
read from the map itself: **flags** - *"flag to capture, nobody holds
it"*, *"enemy flag to capture"*, *"your team's flag"* - and **doors**,
counted as *"2 doors"* or *"one locked door"*, which is the way into a
building. A doorway with no door, an archway or a gap in a wall is not
marked anywhere in a map's data, so those are not on the survey; the
*Openings as you walk* callouts are what find them.

After the things in a square comes **what kind of places it holds**:
the streets and alleys that run through it and which way, a square or
open ground, and each building you can go into - where in the square it
stands, and whether it has an upper floor, a balcony or a rooftop:
*"an alley running east to west, a square, a building to the south-west
with an upper floor"*. It is read from the map's own walls and floors,
the same way as the *Kind of place* callouts as you walk. A house that
nobody can go into is scenery, not a building on the survey, and a place
seen at only a point or two of the square - a doorway, a gap between
roofs - is left out.

A square with nowhere to stand and nothing in it says *"Empty"* rather
than staying silent, because on a grid you cannot see, a key that says
nothing is indistinguishable from a key that did not register.

The direction comes twice over on purpose. *"North-east"* is what you
turn to; *"52 degrees"* is what you hold. It is a true bearing — north
is 0, east is 90 — and it is worked out from the same conversion the
compass callouts use, so the word and the number can never disagree with
each other or with the heading you are already being told.

The squares are the game's own. *"C,3"* is what a sighted teammate reads
off their screen and types into chat, so calling one out means something
to your team without anybody learning anything new.

Four more keys:

- **End** — jump to the next square that has something in it - a thing,
  not only streets and buildings - so you can cross a dead half of the
  map in two presses instead of twelve.
- **Keypad 5** — put the cursor back where you are standing. It is the
  middle of the cluster you are stepping with, which is where "here"
  belongs. **Home** still does the same thing.
- **Page Up** and **Page Down** — change floor on a multi-storey map.
  Without this a roof and a cellar are the same square and the map
  quietly lies to you.

**The survey moved to the keypad in this release**, off the arrow keys it
used to sit on, and it is worth saying what that cost. The keypad arrows
are not free: the stock game puts `selectbuddy` on them, which picks a
fireteam member out by slot number, and five of its eight slots go to
the survey. Keypad End and keypad Home keep slots 0 and 6.

That is a real loss, taken deliberately. `selectbuddy` needs a fireteam,
a roster whose order you know, and a second command after it. The arrow
keys, meanwhile, now do the thing stock ET reaches from no key at all —
turning and looking — which is worth more to a player without a mouse
than five fireteam slots. If you use fireteams heavily, both halves
rebind like any other key.

What you hear is what the game sends your team, and nothing else: your
teammates, the enemies your team has actually *spotted*, the mines it has
detected, the objectives, the spawn points. A disguised covert operative
counts as a teammate, because that is what a sighted player sees. If you
have filtered something off your command map, it is not announced
either. Set `eta_mapCallouts` to 0 to turn the survey off.

One honest limit: the game rounds positions on the command map to about
three metres before sending them. Where the thing is close enough to be
in your view the mod uses its real position instead, but for somebody
spotted across the map, three metres is the best there is.

**If you use the left-handed control layout**, three of these will not
be on their keys. ET's left-handed layout already uses PGUP for look-up,
PGDN for lean-right and END for reload, and the mod never takes a key
you already have bound. Nothing is broken; those three are simply
unbound. The keypad cluster is the same in both layouts, so the four
directions and Keypad 5 arrive as described. Pick three keys you can
spare for the rest:

    bind PGUP       "accessibilityMapUp"
    bind PGDN       "accessibilityMapDown"
    bind END        "accessibilityMapNext"

A sighted player holds the map key and lets go to put the map away.
You do not have to: Accessibility → Environment → *Map key opens and
closes* makes it a switch, and it is on to begin with. Set it to No, or
`eta_mapToggle 0`, to have the map show only while the key is held.

The survey commands do nothing at all unless the map is open, so
whichever keys you give them keep doing their usual job the rest of the
time.

The same goes for the look and turn keys above: the left-handed layout
puts movement on UPARROW, DOWNARROW, LEFTARROW and RIGHTARROW, so the
mod leaves all four alone there. That layout already gives you look-up
on PGUP and look-down on INS; for turning on the spot, bind `+left` and
`+right` to two keys you can spare. HOME is taken there too, which is
why Keypad 5 does the same job.

**You can put down a marker of your own, and be walked to it.** The
game has nothing like this, and a sighted player does not need it: they
look at the far side of the bridge, decide to go there, and go. The
objective guidance cannot stand in for that, because it only ever
points where the map's script says. The marker is a destination you
choose.

- **Keypad 0 places it.** Open the map, walk the cursor to a square
  with the keypad arrows, and press Keypad 0: *"Marker on D,5. 60 meters
  to marker. Turn left toward the marker. Marker lies north-east, you
  are facing north."* Without the map, Keypad 0 marks the spot you are
  standing on - *"Marker set where you are standing"* - so you can find
  your way back to a doorway or a corner after a death or a detour.
- **Keypad dot asks where it is** - the distance, which way to turn, and
  the two compass points.
- **Period (.) turns you toward the way to it**, round the walls, the
  way the ping points: *"Facing the way. 40 meters to marker"*. Walk, and
  press it again whenever you have lost the way. Period was the game's
  zoom of the small map on screen; the mod only takes it if it is still
  on that.
- **Keypad star takes it away.** It also goes by itself when you get
  there: walking into the marked square, or back within three metres of
  a marked spot, says *"Marker reached"*.
- **Keypad plus puts it on the objective.** *"Marker on Command Post.
  40 meters to marker. Marker is 4 meters above you. Turn left toward
  the marker."* The ping then leads you there by the way you can
  actually go - which, for an objective on a floor above, means the
  stairs, the ladder or the ledge, not the wall underneath. When you
  arrive it says *"Marker reached, Command Post"*.

  **Press it again within five seconds for the next objective**, and
  again for the one after: every objective on your team's command map,
  the current one first and then the rest nearest first - command posts,
  gun nests, water pumps, the anti-tank guns, the health and ammo
  cabinets. Each press says which, and where it is in the list: *"Marker
  on Garrison MG Nest, 3 of 7"*. Stop on the one you want and follow the
  ping.

- **Slash puts it on a sniper spot.** A sniper spot is a high place
  you can stand on that looks out a long way: a rooftop, a balcony, a
  ledge, an upper floor window. Press **/** and the marker goes on the
  nearest one, with what it sees: *"Sniper spot 1 of 9: Rooftop, sees 60
  meters to the south-east, 6 meters up. 30 meters to marker."* Press it
  again within five seconds for the next one, further off. The ping then
  leads you there, up the stairs or the ladder, and when you arrive it
  says which way to look: *"Marker reached, sniper spot, look
  south-east"*.

  The spots are found in the map itself - from every raised floor, how
  much of the ground out there a standing enemy could be seen on, how
  far, and how far below - in the first seconds of a map. Asked for
  sooner, it offers the ones found so far, or says it is still looking.

  Some high places that see a long way have no way up: the tops of
  walls, houses nobody can climb. Each spot is checked for a way up in
  the first seconds of a map, while you have no marker out, and one with
  none is left off the list. If you choose a spot before its check, and
  the way to it then turns out to stop underneath it, you hear *"No way
  up to that sniper spot from here"*, the marker goes, and that spot is
  left off the list - press **/** for the next one.

- **Apostrophe puts it on a room.** Press **'** and the marker goes on
  the nearest room you can walk into, with the map's own name for the
  place, which floor it is on and its size: *"Room 1 of 12: Water Pump,
  ground floor, 8 by 6 meters. 25 meters to marker."* Press it again
  within five seconds for the next one, further off. The ping leads you
  there, through the door, and when you arrive you hear *"Marker reached,
  inside the Water Pump"*. A room the map has no name for is said
  *"Room"*. The left-handed layout expands the map on **'**, so the mod
  leaves it alone there: bind `accessibilityRoom` to another key.

  The rooms are found on the same look over the map as the sniper spots:
  every floor with a roof over it, the floors next to each other with no
  wall between them making one room. Rooms nobody can get into - spaces
  the map's builder sealed off - are checked for and left off the list.
  On the command map, each square also says how many rooms it has: *"a
  building to the east, 3 rooms"*.

- **Semicolon puts it on the way out.** Inside a building, press **;**
  and the mod looks for the nearest place you can walk to under the
  open sky - not a skylight, but properly outside - and puts the marker
  there: *"Looking for the way out"*, then *"Way out, 12 meters. Turn
  left toward the marker"*. The ping leads you out through the door, and
  arriving says *"Marker reached, outside"*. Press it again within five
  seconds for another way out, through a different door; *"No other way
  out found"* when there is only the one. Outside, the same key looks
  for the nearest way into a building, into one of the rooms found
  above: *"Way into the Water Pump"*. **;** is free in both of the
  game's layouts.

**The way goes up as well as round.** When the way to the marker climbs
to a floor above, the ping takes you up it:

- **Stairs and ramps** are walked, and said as you come to them: a few
  metres before the foot of a flight on the way you hear *"Stairs up,
  ahead left, 3 meters"* - stairs or a ramp, up or down, which way they
  go from where you face, and how high. Turn that way and walk straight
  up them.
- **A ladder**: the ping leads you to its foot. There the narrator says
  the ladder - *"Ladder up, ahead, 1 meter. F climbs"* - and F climbs
  it. At the top the ping carries on.
- **A ledge too high to step onto** - about a metre, the most a jump
  gets onto: as you reach its edge you hear *"Jump up, ahead, press
  space"* (or *"Jump over"*, for something in the way you jump across),
  with whichever key your jump is on. Walk toward the ping and press it.
- **A ceiling too low to stand under** - a low tunnel or passage: a
  couple of metres before it you hear *"Crouch, low ceiling ahead, press
  c"*, with whichever key your crouch is on. Hold it and walk on. Once
  the way is out from under, *"Stand up, the ceiling is high again"*.
  The way out on **;** goes through low tunnels the same way.

Where there are stairs a little out of the way, the way takes the stairs
rather than a jump: a missed jump costs more than a few metres.

On the way, a soft ping plays about once a second from the way to the
marker. Turn until it is in front of you and walk. The ping changes as
the marker goes round you, so front and back are never confused:

- **In front** - the high ping.
- **To the left or right** - a lower ping, from that side.
- **Behind** - a low double ping. Turn round.

Turn until you hear the high single ping again, then walk. The ping never points
into a wall: several times a second the mod checks that you could walk
toward it, and when a wall is in the way it moves the ping to the
nearest open way out - the one closest to where the marker lies. Behind
that, it works out a whole way you can walk - through the doorway, round
the corner, up the stairs - and the ping sits on the furthest point of
that way you can walk to in a straight line. Reach the corner and it
moves on round it. When the marker itself is in a straight line from
you the ping points at it, and for the last few metres it sits on the
marker itself. A marker on a map square is reached anywhere in the
square.

Keypad dot says the same: *"Turn right toward the marker. The way goes
east. Marker lies north, you are facing north"* - turn right to get
round what is in between, even though the marker is ahead of you.

**Follow the way, not where the marker lies.** *"The way goes"* is the
direction to walk now; *"Marker lies"* is the straight line to the
marker, through walls and floors. They can point opposite ways - the way
may go down a flight of stairs, or round a building, before it turns
back toward the marker - and the ping always follows the way. The way
never points back along the way you came while it goes on ahead, never
off the side of stairs or a balcony, and never through a gap too narrow
for you or under something you would have to crouch under.

The way is worked out from the map's own walls and floors, a moment
after the marker goes down and again every few seconds as you walk, so
it follows you if you wander off it. A closed door counts as a way
through, because it opens. On a big map the whole way can take several
seconds to work out; until then the ping still keeps you off the walls,
walking along them toward the opening nearest the marker.

**The loading screen is said.** Joining a server, you hear who you are
connecting to and what the game is waiting for - *"Connecting to ..."*,
*"Awaiting challenge"*, *"Connected. Awaiting game state"* - and any
file being downloaded, by the quarter. Then the map: *"Loading Oasis.
Objective."* and the mission briefing the loading screen shows, then the
loading bar at 25, 50 and 75 percent, never over the top of the
briefing. When the load is over: *"Loaded. Press L to choose your team
and class"* - with whatever key you have on the team and class menu.
The first time a map is loaded on your machine the bar has nothing to
measure against and the percentages are left out. The distance is also called out as
you close in - *"50 meters to marker"*, *"25 meters to marker"*, with
*"Marker is 4 meters above you"* when it is up or down. These are the
only distances called as you walk: the old *"40 meters to objective"*,
counted in a straight line to the objective's own point, is gone, so
you never hear two numbers for two different places. Put the marker on
the objective with keypad plus to hear its distance. `eta_markerCues 0`, or "Marker sound" on
Accessibility → Environment, turns the ping off and keeps the words.

One marker at a time; placing another moves it. It lasts until you
reach it, clear it, or the map changes, and it survives dying.

The left-handed layout jumps on Keypad 0 (`KP_INS`), so the mod leaves
that key alone there: give Place Marker a key you can spare on
Accessibility → Key Binds.

**Stairs are called, and this release is where they come back.** The
fix above had a cost, and it is only fair to say what it was: lifting
the probe over a stair riser is exactly what stopped stairs being
announced as walls, and it also made them silent. A staircase is
something a sighted player sees from the far side of a room. Before
this you were hearing about them, wrongly; after the fix you heard
nothing, which is the same thing the mod says about an empty corridor.

So the ground ahead is now measured rather than looked at. Flat ground
a short way in front of you, at a height you could actually step to, is
a step — *"Step up"*, *"Step down"*. If the ground beyond it keeps going
the same way, it is a flight of them — *"Stairs up"*, *"Stairs down"*.
Like the drop warning, it looks where you are **moving** when you are
moving and where you are **facing** when you are standing still.

A ramp is left unsaid rather than called stairs. That is deliberate: a
tread is flat, and a slope is not, and sending you to feel for steps
that are not there — in the dark, on a surface you might walk off — is
worse than saying nothing. It rides `eta_environmentCallouts` along with
the rest of the scenery reports.

**What you can use is named, not just clicked at.** When you are close
enough to use something, the game puts an icon under your crosshair.
That icon is how a sighted engineer knows an objective is workable at
all — pliers on a construction, a stick of dynamite where one goes, a
syringe over somebody still worth reviving. Until now the mod answered
all of it with one undifferentiated click.

Now it says which: *"Pliers, build here"*, *"Pliers, defuse here"*,
*"Pliers, arm dynamite here"*, *"Dynamite goes here"*, *"Revive"*, *"Health pack"*, *"Locked door"*,
*"Machine gun"*, and the rest. Where the game draws a progress bar
beside the icon, the number comes with it — *"Pliers, build here, 60
percent"* — so you can tell a construction somebody has nearly finished
from one nobody has touched.

The click has not gone. It still fires the instant an icon appears,
because that is the flicker at the edge of the eye that a sighted player
gets; the words wait a quarter of a second behind it. Turning through a
supply room puts four or five of these under your crosshair inside a
second, and a narrator that read all of them out would be worse than the
click it replaced. Set `eta_interactionCallouts` to 0 to keep the click
and drop the words.

**The build is counted out while you hold the key.** Holding the pliers
on a construction fills a bar on screen, and that bar is how a sighted
engineer knows to keep holding rather than walk away. The tester who
asked for this had finished one and said afterwards that they had only
known to keep holding because they remembered the bar being there.

So the climb is spoken now — *"Building, 27 percent"*, *"Building, 52
percent"*, *"Building, 78 percent"*, *"Building, 91 percent"* — four
times on the way up rather than continuously, for the same reason the
objective distance is: a number that changes every frame is a voice
that never stops. Stop holding and come back later and it counts out
again from where the construction actually stands, so you can tell a
job somebody nearly finished from one nobody has started. The figure is
the game's own, the same number the bar is drawn from, so the words and
the bar cannot disagree.

And when it is done you hear *"Construction complete"*. That is the
game's own event rather than a guess: finishing a task makes it send
the client a "completed" signal for three quarters of a second, and
that signal is what fills the bar to the top on a sighted player's
screen.

What you will never hear is a figure reaching 100. The game resets a
construction's progress to zero in the same frame it finishes, so the
number itself tops out around 97 — which is exactly why completion is
announced as an event and never read out of the bar going quiet. A bar
that stopped arriving means either *built* or *you let go*, and nothing
tells those two apart.

Aim wherever you like while you build. The game stops sending the bar's
number to a player whose crosshair is on their own construction once it
has been started, so looking straight at it used to make the count go
silent. With the pliers out, the mod reads the same number from the
construction itself when the bar's copy is missing. There the finish is
the figure dropping from above ninety percent to nothing in one step,
which the game does only when the work is done - a construction left
alone runs down slowly - so that is announced as *"Construction
complete"* too.

All of it rides `eta_interactionCallouts` with the rest of the hint
narration, and it works on every constructible on every map: the mod
reads the cursor hint the game already sends, so there is nothing
per-map about it.

**Doors creak, and locked ones creak differently.** Every door within
about ten metres has a creaking sound on it, placed where the door
actually is. Turn toward it and it centres; walk away and it fades. It
is not an announcement you can miss — it is still there when you turn
your attention back to it, which is what makes it something you can
navigate by.

A locked door gets a duller, slower creak. Hearing that a door will not
open saves you the walk to it.

The mod does not guess what a door is. The game itself labels doors —
the same label that decides what your cursor says when you point at one
— so lifts, tank barriers and movable bridges do not creak.

**Construction sites tap, demolition targets hiss, and flags ring.**
Three more sounds placed in the world, heard within about twenty-five
metres:

- **A site your team can build** taps like a hammer on steel - two
  light taps and a rest. It is heard while there is something there to
  build, and goes quiet the moment it is finished. A site either team
  can build taps for both.
- **An enemy objective you can blow up** hisses like a lit fuse over a
  low hum: a dynamite target, or something the enemy has built that can
  be destroyed - a bridge, a command post, a machine gun nest. It goes
  quiet once it is destroyed.
- **A flag you can capture** - like the Old City flag on Oasis - rings
  a two-note bell, high then low. It rings while your team does not
  hold it and goes quiet once you have raised it. A flag the map keeps
  for the other side never rings for you. Unlike the other two, a flag
  is heard while it is in view of where you are, which in the open is
  most of the way there.
- **The tank to escort** - the Jagdpanther on Goldrush, which the
  Allies steal from the Axis - rumbles like a labouring engine, with its
  treads rattling, wherever it is and for both sides: one side walks
  beside it to move it, the other stops it. When it is damaged and it is
  your side's to escort, it taps like a construction site instead - an
  engineer repairs it with pliers.

They come from the same markers the game puts on the compass and the
command map, so they mean what those icons mean: a construction that
cannot be destroyed never hisses. Your own team's objectives stay
silent - they are there to be defended, and a sound on each would fill
your base. Armed dynamite already ticks in the game itself, for
everybody.

Like the doors, these are heard through walls: they say what is near,
and the walk there is yours. To hear them before you play, open the `sounds`
folder in the download: `build_site.wav` is the taps, and
`demolition_target.wav` the hiss, `flag_capture.wav` the bell, `tank.wav`
the engine. The door, marker and crosshair sounds are there too.
*Construction, demolition, flag and tank sounds* on the Environment page
turns them off.

**Things to pick up are heard where they lie.** Each health pack, ammo
pack and weapon within about ten metres and in sight plays its own
sound from where it lies, the nearest three at a time, so you can turn
to it and walk over it: two soft rising notes for a health pack, three
dry clicks for an ammo pack, one heavy clank for a gun. A dropped
objective - the gold on Goldrush, the documents - rings the flag's bell.
Only what you could see is heard, never through a wall. In the
`sounds` folder they are `health_pack.wav`, `ammo_pack.wav` and
`weapon_ground.wav`. *Pickup sounds* on the Environment page turns them
off, and *Pickup sounds volume* on the Sound page sets how loud.

**Landmines are warned of.** A landmine the game shows you is said as
you come within about ten metres, with where and how far - *"Enemy
landmine, ahead, 6 meters"*, or *"3 enemy landmines"* for several
together - and within about six metres it beeps, two fast high beeps
from where it lies, so you hear it as you step round it. Your own
team's are *"Friendly landmine"*: a teammate sets one off too. The
marker's way steps round every mine you have been shown, and finds a
new way when one comes into sight on it; where there is no way round,
it goes through, and the warning says where the mine is.

Only the mines a sighted player would see. An enemy mine is hidden in
the ground once it is armed, and shows once someone on your team has
spotted it - a covert op, or a field op's binoculars - or, with the
battle sense skill that shows traps, when you are close to it. One
nobody has spotted stays hidden, as it is from everyone. In the
`sounds` folder the beep is `landmine.wav`. *Landmine warnings* on the
Environment page turns them off; the beep follows *Alert sounds volume*
on the Sound page.

**Walking into something is heard.** Walk into a wall and a dull thud
sounds from where you hit; walk into a thing - a door, a crate, a
vehicle, a player - and a hollow knock. Once as you hit, and again
about twice a second while you keep pushing; walking along a wall, or
up a step, makes none. In the `sounds` folder they are `bump_wall.wav`
and `bump_object.wav`. *Bump sounds* on the Environment page turns them
off, and *Bump sounds volume* on the Sound page sets how loud.

**How you died is said.** When you die, the game's own words come with
*"Down, a medic can revive you"*: *"You failed to spot Hans's Landmine"*,
*"You were killed by Hans's MP 40"*. Your kills too: *"You killed
Hans"*. Everyone else's deaths stay in the kill feed.

**Spectating or in play is said.** When it changes, the narrator says
which: *"Spectating"*, or *"Spectating, following"* and the name of the
player you are watching; *"In play, Allies, Medic"* when you spawn;
*"Down, a medic can revive you"*; *"Waiting to respawn"*, with who you
are watching meanwhile; and *"Match over"*. The status key, F5, opens
with the same words - while you watch somebody, the health and weapon
that follow are theirs.

**Who you watch is said.** When the view lands on somebody, you hear
who, their side and class, and how they are: *"Spectating, following
Hans, Axis Medic, 85 health"*. After that only what happens to them:
what they kill and what kills them, the weapon they change to, and
*"Hans revived"* when a medic gets them up.

**The console says when it opens.** The key beside 1 opens the game's
console, where everything typed goes until it is closed. *"Console open.
The same key closes it"* when it opens, *"Console closed"* when it
closes, in a match and in the menus.

**Ladders are said, and F climbs them.** A ladder is said as you come
within two metres of it, with which way it goes - *"Ladder down, ahead
left, 2 meters. F climbs"* - once, until you walk away. Press **F** and
the mod climbs it for you: it turns you to the ladder, looks the way
the game needs you to look, and walks you on - up to the top and a step
onto the ledge, or down to the bottom - saying *"Top of the ladder"* or
*"Bottom of the ladder"* when you are there. Going down from the top
means stepping off the edge onto the ladder, the way the game always
has, and the mod does that too.

Press F again, or turn by hand, to stop a climb part way. The climb
aims for the middle of the ladder, not its edge, and if it could not get
onto the ladder within five seconds it stops and says so. F climbs only when the ladder is nearer than anything else there is
to use; with a door or a cabinet closer, F turns to that as before.

**Finding and arming your dynamite.** Dynamite is thrown, not placed:
it leaves your hand low and can roll. Once it stops, the narrator says
where it lies - *"Dynamite down, 2 meters ahead left, not armed. Take
pliers and press F"*. Then:

1. Switch to your pliers.
2. Press F. The view turns to the dynamite - *"Your dynamite, not
   armed, ahead, 2 meters"*.
3. Walk up until you are standing right by it; crouch if you are not
   sure. The pliers reach only about a metre and a half from your eyes.
4. When the pliers are on it you hear *"Pliers, arm dynamite here"*.
   Hold fire until the game says the dynamite is armed.

F only turns an engineer to dynamite, and only to dynamite you can see.
An enemy's armed dynamite is on the same list - *"Enemy dynamite,
armed"* - and over it the pliers say *"Pliers, defuse here"*.

**Ladders knock.** Every ladder within about fifteen metres - the
nearest two at a time - plays three knocks climbing wooden rungs,
placed on the ladder itself: level with you on a ladder up a wall, at
the top of one going down a shaft. Turn until it is in front of you and
walk to it; when you are there, the narrator says the ladder and F
climbs it. The knocking stops while you climb.

**F turns you to a ladder, too.** A ladder you can see, within about
fifteen metres, is on F's list with the other things to use: press F and
the view turns to it - *"Ladder up, ahead left, 6 meters"*. Walk to it
and press F again to climb. If something else is nearer, F turns to that
first; press again within a moment for the next along, as always.

**F turns you to stairs, too.** A flight of stairs within about ten
metres - neat steps, or steps cut in the rock - is on F's list: press F
and the view turns to the foot of the stairs - *"Stairs up, 3 meters,
ahead left, 4 meters"*, which way up or down and how high, then where
the foot is. Walk to it and press F again: standing at the foot, F turns
you to face straight up the flight, or straight down it from the top -
*"Facing up the stairs. Walk forward"* - and walking forward climbs
them. Nothing is pressed: stairs are walked. Something to use within
reach still comes before the stairs; **]** and **[** reach the stairs
too.

The map has nothing that marks stairs, so they are looked for as F's
list is made: out from you in sixteen directions, the floor followed for
about fourteen metres, a run of sharp rises a step high with footing
between them. A sandy slope rises as much, but evenly, and is not
stairs.

A ladder is not something the game sends to the player - it is part of
the map's walls - so the mod reads every ladder out of the map file
itself as the map loads. That is why a ladder round a corner is heard
before you reach it. Like the doors, ladders are heard through walls:
they say what is there, and the walk is yours. `ladder.wav` in the
`sounds` folder is the knocks, and *Ladder sounds* on the Environment
page turns them off. The command map survey counts the ladders in each
square too.

**The crosshair ticks when it is on somebody.** Bright for an enemy, low
and soft for a teammate. It repeats about six times a second, which is
deliberate: sweeping across a target may put it under your crosshair for
only a fraction of a second, and a slower cue would simply miss it.

Two things worth knowing about it. It reads the game's own crosshair, so
it can never tell you about somebody through a wall. And a disguised
covert operative sounds like a **teammate** — because that is what a
sighted player sees, and the game is deceiving them on purpose. The one
exception is a Field Ops with the enemy-recognition skill, where the
game itself reveals the disguise; then so does this.

Being honest about that one: the stock game shows a sighted player a
*teammate's* name under the crosshair and says nothing at all for an
enemy — they know because they can see a person standing there. So the
teammate tick matches a real feature of the game, and the enemy tick
restores what sight gives rather than what the display gives. Set
`eta_crosshairCues` to 0 if you would rather not have it.

**Where you are, in words your team will recognise.** Most maps ship a
list of named places, and the mod reads the same one the game uses to
fill in your location when a sighted player types in team chat. So you
hear *"Old City Wall"* or *"Axis Command Post"* — and if you pass that
on, your team knows exactly where you mean. Maps with no such list fall
back to the command map's grid square (*"grid D,4"*), which is what a
sighted player would read off the map anyway, so you always get an
answer.

It waits until you have actually stopped somewhere. A stock map names
over a hundred places and you walk through several of them crossing a
courtyard — announcing each would be a voice that never stops. Arriving
somewhere is the event, not passing through. Press the status key and
it tells you immediately, without the wait.

The status key also says whether you have **open sky above you** or are
**under cover**. That is deliberately not "indoors" and "outdoors": the
mod can prove open sky, because the game flags the sky itself, but a
ceiling might be a roof, a bridge, a tunnel or an awning and it will not
guess between them. It matters in play — an airstrike or a mortar round
needs open sky to come through. Set `eta_locationCallouts` to 0 to
silence the automatic announcement; the status key still reports it.

**The drop warning, and what it is worth.** When the ground in front of
you falls away you hear *"Ledge ahead, 2 metres down"*, *"Drop ahead"*
for one that would injure you, or *"Deadly drop ahead"* for one that
would kill you outright. Those last two are not a judgement of mine:
the game works out falling damage from the speed you land at, and those
are its own two thresholds turned back into heights, using the gravity
in play on that server. If a server changes gravity, the warning moves
with it.

It probes one stride ahead, in the direction you are **moving** when you
are moving and the direction you are **facing** when you are standing
still - because a hole you are about to walk into matters more than one
you happen to be looking at, and standing still is when "what is over
there" is the question.

Two honest limits. It only reports what is directly ahead, so backing
off an edge is not covered. And when there is water at the bottom, it
says so *only* for a drop that could not have hurt you anyway - the
engine does cancel falling damage for a player who lands submerged, but
a probe cannot tell deep water from a puddle on the floor of a deep
shaft, and "jump, there is water down there" is the one thing here that
must never be said on a guess.

**Water tells you what you can do, not what it looks like.** *"Shallow
water"*, *"Deep water"*, *"Swimming"*, and *"Out of the water"*. The
middle two are the game's own thresholds: at *deep* a jump becomes a
swim instead of a jump, and at *swimming* you are under and falling
damage stops applying to you entirely.

**The compass waits for you to stop turning.** A tester reported it
lagging behind the mouse. It was not slow - it was behind, because a
sweep across half the compass crosses four headings in a few frames and
each one was announced. Speech cannot deliver four reports in the time
your hand takes to move, so they either queued up behind each other or
cut each other off. Now a heading has to be held briefly before it is
spoken, so a sweep says where you ended up and says it once. A slow,
deliberate turn is unaffected and still names each point as you pass
through it. Set `eta_facingCallouts` to 0 to silence it entirely.

**The scenery waits when you have asked a question.** Speech has one
voice, and a new line is delivered by cutting off the one in progress.
So pressing the status key or the objectives key now keeps the scenery
quiet for a few seconds afterwards — long enough for the longest answer
the mod gives. Being talked over while asking a question is worse than
missing a wall.

There is one exception, and it is deliberate: the **drop warning** is
never held back. The single thing worth interrupting an answer for is
being told you are about to walk off something that will kill you.
Pressing the stop-speech key clears the wait as well, since asking for
silence should not mean silence followed by more silence.

**A zero is not a fault.** The ammo report says what the game says,
including when that is nothing. A medic spawns *"30 loaded, 0 in
reserve"* - Thompson for the Allies, MP 40 for the Axis, both the same
- because the game gives the medic one clip less than everyone else.
The engine's own class table says so in as many words: "medic start
with 1 clip less". Spare clips come from a Field Ops ammo pack, or from
the Light Weapons skill once it reaches the extra ammo level. Sighted
players read the same zero off the HUD, so there is nothing wrong with
the mod when you hear it.

**Text boxes read back.** Arriving at one says its label, that it
is a text box, and what it currently holds - *"Player name, edit box,
Alex"*, or *"empty"*. Every character you type is spoken as it lands,
and backspace says which character it removed. Left and right arrows
read the field back one character at a time, and say *"end"* when you
reach it.

Space and punctuation are spoken by name - *"space"*, *"dot"*,
*"colon"*, *"slash"* - because a space read as itself is silence, and
silence is indistinguishable from the key not registering. That matters
most for server addresses.

**Passwords are never read aloud.** The password, rcon password and
server join password boxes say how many characters they hold, and
*"star"* as you type. Two of those the game already hides on screen; the
server join password on the host-game screen it does not, but a password
read out loud in a room is not the same as one shown on your own
monitor, so speech keeps it quiet regardless.


**Play Online can be heard.** The filter boxes beside the server name
box say what they filter and how they are set, the server list says each
server as you move through it - name, map, game type, players and bots,
ping, and what its icons show, such as a password - and when a refresh
ends you hear how many servers were found. Every list in the menus is
read the same way.

**Each menu says which it is.** The main menu says *"Main menu"* when
you start the game, after the update check's answer, and again when you
leave a match. In a match, Escape says *"In-game menu"*, and the team
and class panel says *"Limbo menu"* as it opens.

**Updates come to you.** As soon as the main menu is up, the game says
*"Checking for updates"* and asks the mod's public downloads page for a
newer version. The answer is said every time: *"You have the latest
version"*, or *"Could not check for updates"* - with no internet, say -
each followed by *"Main menu"*, which is where you are. If there is a
new version, a popup reads out the offer - *"A new version is
available"* - with two answers:
*Download* or *Later*. Download fetches the new mod and checks it
against the checksum the release published; then a second popup offers
*Restart now* or *When I quit*. The new version is put in place once
the game has closed, so either way the next start runs it. Nothing is
replaced without you choosing Download, and a download that arrives
damaged is thrown away and your version stays as it was.

The downloads page is
github.com/alexzgeorgian/Wolfenstein-Enemy-territory-accessibility-releases.
It holds only the releases, so anyone can download from it without a
GitHub account, and it is the only place the game asks.

When a new version is built for a newer ET: Legacy than yours, the
popup says so instead and asks you to update ET: Legacy first, with a
button that opens the ET: Legacy download page. Those releases jump
the version number - beta-0.02 to beta-0.03, say - so they stand out.

*Options*, *Accessibility* has the rest: *Check for updates at start*
turns the start-up check off, and *Check for updates* asks now and also
tells you when you already have the newest. The check needs the
internet; a test build never checks.

<!-- windows -->
The game puts the updated mod in `Documents\ETLegacy\legacy`, which it
reads before the folder where it is installed, so no administrator
permission is needed. The previous version is kept beside it as
`zzz_accessibility.pk3.previous`. If you later install a build by hand,
put it in that same Documents folder, or delete the copy there - a pk3
in Documents wins over one where the game is installed.
<!-- /windows -->
<!-- mac -->
The game puts the updated mod where you installed it, in
`~/Library/Application Support/etlegacy/legacy`, once the game has
closed. The previous version is kept beside it as
`zzz_accessibility.pk3.previous`. With *Restart now*, the game opens
again by itself.
<!-- /mac -->

## 7. Checking what you downloaded

`SHA256SUMS` sits next to the zips on the release page, and covers the
downloads themselves, one line for each.
<!-- windows -->

    certutil -hashfile @ARCHIVE@ SHA256

and compare it against the line in `SHA256SUMS` for that file.
<!-- /windows -->
<!-- mac -->

In Terminal, in the folder you downloaded to:

    shasum -a 256 @MAC_ARCHIVE@

and compare it against the line in `SHA256SUMS` for that file.
<!-- /mac -->

`SHA256SUMS.contents`, inside the unzipped folder, lists the
libraries within the pk3, for anyone who wants to look that far. The pk3
is an ordinary zip, so extract it and run
`sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS.contents`.

`release/build-release.sh` in this repository rebuilds the package from
source if you would rather not trust a binary.


## 8. Uninstalling

<!-- windows -->
Delete `zzz_accessibility.pk3` from the `legacy` folder where the game
is installed, and `autoexec.cfg` from `Documents\ETLegacy\legacy`. If
the game has updated the mod for you, also delete `zzz_accessibility.pk3`
and `zzz_accessibility.pk3.previous` from `Documents\ETLegacy\legacy`.
<!-- /windows -->
<!-- mac -->
Delete `zzz_accessibility.pk3`, `zzz_accessibility.pk3.previous`,
`autoexec.cfg` and `qagame_mac` from
`~/Library/Application Support/etlegacy/legacy`, and `cgame_mac` and
`ui_mac`, which the game unpacked there, and the `omni-bot` folder if you
set up bots.
<!-- /mac -->
If you pasted the lines onto an `autoexec.cfg` you already had, delete
those lines rather than the file.

Nothing else was changed, and the unzipped folder can go too.


## 9. What is worth telling me

The bugs that automated testing cannot reach are the ones about timing
and judgement, so those are the useful ones:

- Did the limbo panel get you into a match? Which key you were on when
  it did or did not, and what it said, is the useful detail.
- Does anything speak at the wrong moment, or too often to follow?
- Does narration talk over your screen reader, or interrupt itself?
- When it says turn left, is the objective actually on your left?
- Does the damage direction cue match where you were shot from?
- Is anything important silent that should not be?
